<<<
Workless gives another example. We have all known ‘ the ~ unemployed’ as long as we can. remember. But unemployed fills up a good deal of headline; something shorter is wanted, & workless is invented for the need.
===
*Workless* gives another example. We have all known 'the unemployed' as long as we can remember. But *unemployed* fills up a good deal of headline; something shorter is wanted, & *workless* is invented for the need.
>>>

<<<
But, secondly, workless by itself is shorter than the workless; so workless is turned from an adjective into an indeclinable plural noun—all to make possible such gems as: KENT WORKLESS WANT TO

SHE PREMIER

TO AID WORKLESS
===
But, secondly, *workless* by itself is shorter than *the workless*; so *workless* is turned from an adjective into an indeclinable plural noun—all to make possible such gems as:

KENT WORKLESS WANT TO SEE PREMIER

TO AID WORKLESS
>>>

