<<<
worldly. So spelt, not wordly; but the mistake is common; cf. unwieldly for unwieldy.
===
**worldly.** So spelt, not *wordly*; but the mistake is common; cf. *unwieldly* for *unwieldy*.
>>>

<<<
WORN-OUT HUMOUR. t We are not amused’; so Queen Victoria baldly stated a fact that was disconcerting to someone; yet the thing was very likely amusing in its nature; it did not amuse the person whose amusement mattered, that was all.
===
**WORN-OUT HUMOUR.** 'We are not amused'; so Queen Victoria baldly stated a fact that was disconcerting to someone; yet the thing was very likely amusing in its nature; it did not amuse the person whose amusement mattered, that was all.
>>>

<<<
The writer’s Queen Victoria is his public, & he would do well to keep a bust of the old Queen on his desk with the legend ‘ We are not amused’ hanging from it.
===
The writer's Queen Victoria is his public, & he would do well to keep a bust of the old Queen on his desk with the legend 'We are not amused' hanging from it.
>>>
